-고 把两个动作并列或按顺序连起来；\\n-아/어/여서 表示原因、理由，或前后.en:
-고 connects two actions in parallel or in sequence;
-아/어/여서 indicates cause or reason, or a natural flow between actions.
This lesson is the starting point of "complex sentences." Once you learn these two connectors, you can expand single sentences into coherent expressions.
In Chinese, "eat and drink" uses "and" directly, but Korean uses -고 attached to the verb stem;
In Chinese, "because...so" are separate words, but Korean's -아/어서 attaches directly to the verb, with no separate "because" word.

名词+이다 做谓语；\\n动词 + -는 것 把动作名词化；\\n形容词 + -.en:
Noun + 이다 acts as the predicate;
Verb + -는 것 nominalizes the action;
Adjective + -게 becomes an adverb, indicating manner.
This lesson is about Korean's "transformation tools" that let verbs be used as subjects and adjectives modify actions.
In Chinese, verbs can directly serve as subjects ("学习很重要"), but in Korean, you must use -는 것 to nominalize the verb before placing it in the subject or object position.

三块内容：①명사+이에요（是……，已学基础）；\\n②-는 것（动作名词化，\".en:
Three parts: ① Noun + 이에요 (it is..., already learned as a base);
② -는 것 (action nominalization, "the act of...");
③ -게 (adjective to adverb, "simply," "quickly").
The focus is on the latter two, which make sentence structures more flexible.

이/가 아니에요 表示\"不是\"；\\n이/가 아니라 B예요 表示\"不是A.en:
이/가 아니에요 means "is not"; 
이/가 아니라 B예요 means "not A but B"; 
-는 게 아니라 is used to correct actions. 
These three structures are all used to "clarify the truth"—correcting misunderstandings, clarifying facts, and contrasting two statements. 
In Chinese, "not A but B" is expressed through word order and separate words, while Korean uses the dedicated structure 이/가 아니라 + B예요, and the particle choice depends on whether there is a final consonant.

三块内容：①이/가 아니에요（单纯否定\"不是\"）；\\n②이/가 아니라 B.en:
Three parts: ①이/가 아니에요 (simple negation "is not"); 
②이/가 아니라 B예요 (contrastive negation "not A but B"); 
③-는 게 아니라 (action-level "not doing A"). 
아니라 must be followed by the correct answer; it cannot be used alone.

三套冠词形：①动词现在→-는（공부하는 사람）；\\n②形容词→-은/ㄴ（按收音.en:
Three sets of adnominal forms: ①verb present → -는 (공부하는 사람); 
②adjective → -은/ㄴ (based on final consonant, 어려운 문법); 
③verb future/incomplete → -을/ㄹ (갈 곳). 
After transformation, place directly before the noun—the structure is the same as Chinese "...的 + noun."

这节课是上节课 타다/(으)로 가다 的延伸，核心动词换成 갈아타다。\\n三种.en:
This lesson extends the previous lesson on 타다/(으)로 가다, with the core verb changed to 갈아타다.
Three expressions: ①을/를 갈아타요 (transfer to a certain vehicle);
②(으)로 갈아타요 (change to a certain direction);
③A에서 B로 갈아타요 (transfer from A to B, the most complete form).
